    "body": "One in six leaders say an AI tool mishandled supplier or customer data at their company in the past year, according to new research from InstaLILY. That figure rose to 22% among supply chain respondents.\nWhat’s Related\nMost respondents (81%) reported no incident, while 3% were unsure. The research did not establish how serious the incidents were or whether data left the business.\nThe findings come from a Pollfish survey launched Oct. 9 of 250 managers and executives in supply chain, logistics, distribution, and wholesale whose companies use AI in daily work.\nDoes AI have too much info?\nThe findings show how far AI has reached into commercial data. Seventy-eight percent of respondents said their AI tools can access customer pricing and margins, and the same share said AI can reach customer purchase histories. Sixty-seven percent said their tools can access supplier pricing and rebate data.\nConfidence is high anyway. Eighty-seven percent said they are very or somewhat confident their AI tools keep supplier and customer data separated, even though only 32% said that data sits in separate systems.\nRELATED STORIES: 1 in 4 IT Leaders Say AI Mistakes Have Hurt Their Business | Procurement Leaders See AI Skills as Essential, but Few Feel Ready\nCustomers and suppliers are paying attention. 63% said their company has been asked how it protects supplier or customer data. Of those, 47% said the question affected a decision about a vendor, software or AI tool.\nThe top barrier to keeping data separate was tools not built with that in mind (22%), followed closely by cost (21%) and a lack of technical expertise (20%). 90% said they would be more likely to choose AI built for distribution and supply chain over a general-purpose tool if it offered better control over that data. InstaLILY sells AI agents for distributors and logistics companies.\nAbout half said their employer would bear the most responsibility for an exposure, compared with 23% who pointed to the AI vendor.\nQuestions to Ask Before Expanding AI\nThe report lays out several steps for companies widening their use of AI:\nMap what each tool can reach. That includes AI features built into existing software, not just standalone tools.\nTest the permissions. Can an employee pull up account terms they shouldn’t see? Could a customer quote reveal a confidential supplier rebate?\nMake vendors prove it. Ask providers to run a realistic workflow with test data and tell you what records they can access when investigating an incident.\nHave an answer ready. Prepare clear explanations for suppliers and customers who ask how you handle their information.\nReview access before consolidating. Seventy-eight percent rate cutting down to fewer AI vendors as a high or moderate priority for the next year. The report advises reassessing who needs access before moving workflows to a new platform.",
